// meteor-cfd case - SPEC-LIT §46/§47, run by `ofgpu-cht`.
//
// A four-layer semiconductor package, in section: a silicon die dissipating
// 100 W over a 10 x 10 mm footprint, a solder TIM1, a copper heat spreader,
// and a grease TIM2 to an isothermal cold plate. Everything here is
// conduction: the interfaces carry SPEC-LIT §47.5's contact resistances, and
// the die's metallisation is a mesh-axis-diagonal anisotropic conductivity
// (§46.3's Wiener pair for a layered stack).
//
// WHAT THIS CASE IS FOR. It is the semiconductor thermal-analysis problem the
// §47 work opens, reduced to the part that has a closed form. The whole stack
// is one-dimensional in z, so the junction temperature is a series sum:
//
//   q = 100 W / 1e-4 m^2 = 1e6 W/m^2
//
//   below the die's bottom face, m^2 K/W:
//     Rc(die/solder)      1.0e-5
//     solder    5e-5/57   8.771930e-7
//     Rc(solder/spreader) 2.5e-5
//     spreader  2e-3/398  5.025126e-6
//     Rc(spreader/grease) 4.0e-5      (written as thicknessLayers/kappaLayers)
//     grease    9e-4/3.5  2.571429e-4
//                         ----------
//                         3.380452e-4   ->  338.0452 K
//
//   inside the die, where the source is lumped per cell (this is the FINITE
//   VOLUME die, not the analytic one - with a uniform source the two differ
//   at second order, and what this case gates is that the solver reproduces
//   its own discretisation exactly):
//     q (h/2)/k_z  +  (g h^2/k_z)(1 + 2 + 3)   with h = 0.7 mm / 4, k_z = 30
//     = 2.9167 + 8.7500                        ->   11.6667 K
//
//   T_junction = 300 + 338.0452 + 11.6667 = 649.7118 K
//
// MEASURED, on an RTX 5070 Ti in f64: T_junction = 649.7118 K, each of the
// three interfaces carries exactly -100.000000 / +100.000000 W, the §47.12
// Gate 4 conservation imbalance is 0.000e0, and the largest interface
// temperature JUMP is 40.000000 K = q * 4e-5 - the third contact resistance,
// visible in the reported face values because §47.3's Robin triple can carry
// a discontinuity and a cyclic interpolation could not.
// `io::case_cht::tests::the_shipped_die_stack_case_matches_its_closed_form`
// asserts all of that against the formula above, rebuilt from these numbers
// rather than transcribed.
//
// WHAT IT IS NOT. There is no air, no water and no flow: the cold plate is an
// isothermal boundary, not a fluid region. SPEC-LIT §47's fluid side is
// implemented and tested in `crate::cht` (the wall-function conductance of
// §47.6, the two limits of §47.12's Gate 2) but no case format reaches it
// yet, and `ofgpu-cht` refuses a `"kind": "fluid"` region by name rather than
// building a solid and calling it one.
//
// THE MECHANICS HALF (SPEC-LIT §96). After the conduction solve converges,
// the die, the solder and the spreader each have SPEC-LIT §95's linear
// thermo-elastic displacement solved on their own mesh, from their own
// dT = T - TRef. The elastic numbers are ROUND REPRESENTATIVE VALUES - no
// handbook was transcribed, and this case gates that the chain runs and
// writes, not a stress number. The one cited source is silicon's expansion
// coefficient: Okada & Tokumaru, J. Appl. Phys. 56 (1984) 314, DOI
// 10.1063/1.333965 (alpha_Si = 2.6e-6 /K at 300 K). Three idealisations are
// stated because they are all load-bearing:
//
// 1. Every interface face of a mechanical region carries
//    `fixedDisplacement [null, null, 0.0]` - the frictionless-rigid-plane
//    idealisation of a bond: the face may slide, it may not lift. Its
//    consequence: `u_z = 0` on BOTH faces of the solder and of the
//    spreader suppresses their through-thickness expansion entirely.
//    The faithful alternative is one region with a `materials` zone list
//    (docs/10's R4; `bimetalStrip.cht.jsonc` is that route).
// 2. `*SideXMin` and `*SideYMin` are `symmetry`: the modelled 10 x 10 mm is
//    ONE QUADRANT of a 20 x 20 mm package mirrored in both planes. Without
//    them x- and y-translation and the rotation about z are unconstrained,
//    and the displacement operator refuses the singular component systems
//    by name (SPEC-LIT §95.2).
// 3. The peak stress the run prints is a LINEAR-ELASTIC number on a joint
//    that in reality yields: read it as a comparison between cases, not as
//    a margin against a proof stress.
//
// The grease carries no `mechanics`: a grease joint has no stiffness worth
// modelling, and the region writes its temperature only.
{
  "name": "dieStack",

  "regions": [
    {
      // The die. 10 x 10 x 0.7 mm of silicon, dissipating 100 W uniformly:
      // 100 W / (1e-2 * 1e-2 * 7e-4 m^3) = 1.428571e9 W/m^3.
      //
      // kappa is ANISOTROPIC and diagonal in the mesh axes - SPEC-LIT §46.3's
      // layered homogenisation. In-plane the metallisation raises the
      // effective conductivity; through-plane the dielectric layers lower it.
      // The stack axis is z, so the through-plane value is the z component.
      // §46.4 accepts this because the mesh is axis-aligned: `K Sf` is then
      // parallel to `Sf` and the anisotropy residual is identically zero.
      "name": "die",
      "mesh": {
        "bounds": { "min": [0.0, 0.0, 0.00295], "max": [0.010, 0.010, 0.00365] },
        "cells": [10, 10, 4],
        "boundaries": {
          "xmin": "dieSideXMin", "xmax": "dieSideXMax",
          "ymin": "dieSideYMin", "ymax": "dieSideYMax",
          "zmin": "dieToSolder", "zmax": "dieTop"
        }
      },
      "material": { "rho": 2330.0, "c": 700.0, "kappa": [120.0, 120.0, 30.0] },
      "source": 1.428571428571429e9,
      "patches": [
        // The package lid is not modelled; the die's top and edges are
        // adiabatic, which is the conservative assumption for a
        // junction-temperature estimate.
        { "match": "dieTop",       "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "dieSideXMin",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "dieSideXMax",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "dieSideYMin",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "dieSideYMax",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } }
      ],
      "mechanics": {
        "material": { "E": 130e9, "nu": 0.28, "alpha": 2.6e-6, "TRef": 300.0 },
        "patches": [
          { "match": "dieToSolder",   "u": { "type": "fixedDisplacement", "value": [null, null, 0.0] } },
          { "match": "dieSideXMin",   "u": { "type": "symmetry" } },
          { "match": "dieSideYMin",   "u": { "type": "symmetry" } },
          { "match": "dieSideXMax",   "u": { "type": "free" } },
          { "match": "dieSideYMax",   "u": { "type": "free" } },
          { "match": "dieTop",        "u": { "type": "free" } }
        ],
        "solver": { "tolerance": 1e-6, "maxOuter": 2000 }
      }
    },

    {
      // TIM1: a 50 um solder layer. Meshed as its own region rather than
      // folded into a contact resistance, because SPEC-LIT (S47.13)'s
      // zero-thickness test - Fo = alpha t / t_TIM^2 >> 1 - is what decides
      // between the two, and a solder joint has enough heat capacity to
      // matter in a transient.
      "name": "solder",
      "mesh": {
        "bounds": { "min": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0029], "max": [0.010, 0.010, 0.00295] },
        "cells": [10, 10, 2],
        "boundaries": {
          "xmin": "solderSideXMin", "xmax": "solderSideXMax",
          "ymin": "solderSideYMin", "ymax": "solderSideYMax",
          "zmin": "solderToSpreader", "zmax": "solderToDie"
        }
      },
      "material": { "rho": 7400.0, "c": 220.0, "kappa": 57.0 },
      "patches": [
        { "match": "solderSideXMin", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "solderSideXMax", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "solderSideYMin", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "solderSideYMax", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } }
      ],
      "mechanics": {
        "material": { "E": 30e9, "nu": 0.40, "alpha": 24e-6, "TRef": 300.0 },
        "patches": [
          { "match": "solderToSpreader", "u": { "type": "fixedDisplacement", "value": [null, null, 0.0] } },
          { "match": "solderToDie",      "u": { "type": "fixedDisplacement", "value": [null, null, 0.0] } },
          { "match": "solderSideXMin",   "u": { "type": "symmetry" } },
          { "match": "solderSideYMin",   "u": { "type": "symmetry" } },
          { "match": "solderSideXMax",   "u": { "type": "free" } },
          { "match": "solderSideYMax",   "u": { "type": "free" } }
        ],
        "solver": { "tolerance": 1e-6, "maxOuter": 2000 }
      }
    },

    {
      // The copper heat spreader, 2 mm.
      "name": "spreader",
      "mesh": {
        "bounds": { "min": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0009], "max": [0.010, 0.010, 0.0029] },
        "cells": [10, 10, 8],
        "boundaries": {
          "xmin": "spreaderSideXMin", "xmax": "spreaderSideXMax",
          "ymin": "spreaderSideYMin", "ymax": "spreaderSideYMax",
          "zmin": "spreaderToGrease", "zmax": "spreaderToSolder"
        }
      },
      "material": { "rho": 8960.0, "c": 385.0, "kappa": 398.0 },
      "patches": [
        { "match": "spreaderSideXMin", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "spreaderSideXMax", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "spreaderSideYMin", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "spreaderSideYMax", "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } }
      ],
      "mechanics": {
        "material": { "E": 120e9, "nu": 0.34, "alpha": 17e-6, "TRef": 300.0 },
        "patches": [
          { "match": "spreaderToGrease",  "u": { "type": "fixedDisplacement", "value": [null, null, 0.0] } },
          { "match": "spreaderToSolder",  "u": { "type": "fixedDisplacement", "value": [null, null, 0.0] } },
          { "match": "spreaderSideXMin",  "u": { "type": "symmetry" } },
          { "match": "spreaderSideYMin",  "u": { "type": "symmetry" } },
          { "match": "spreaderSideXMax",  "u": { "type": "free" } },
          { "match": "spreaderSideYMax",  "u": { "type": "free" } }
        ],
        "solver": { "tolerance": 1e-6, "maxOuter": 2000 }
      }
    },

    {
      // TIM2: 0.9 mm of thermal grease down to the cold plate, whose face is
      // held at 300 K.
      "name": "grease",
      "mesh": {
        "bounds": { "min": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0], "max": [0.010, 0.010, 0.0009] },
        "cells": [10, 10, 3],
        "boundaries": {
          "xmin": "greaseSideXMin", "xmax": "greaseSideXMax",
          "ymin": "greaseSideYMin", "ymax": "greaseSideYMax",
          "zmin": "coldPlate", "zmax": "greaseToSpreader"
        }
      },
      "material": { "rho": 2600.0, "c": 900.0, "kappa": 3.5 },
      "patches": [
        { "match": "coldPlate",       "T": { "type": "fixedValue", "value": 300.0 } },
        { "match": "greaseSideXMin",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "greaseSideXMax",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "greaseSideYMin",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } },
        { "match": "greaseSideYMax",  "T": { "type": "zeroGradient" } }
      ]
    }
  ],

  // SPEC-LIT §47.5. `Rc` is what ASTM D5470 reports as the intercept of its
  // R_total-versus-thickness line: the two mating surfaces' contact
  // resistances, with the layer's own bulk resistance already carried by the
  // meshed region.
  "interfaces": [
    {
      "regionA": "die",      "patchA": "dieToSolder",
      "regionB": "solder",   "patchB": "solderToDie",
      "Rc": 1.0e-5
    },
    {
      "regionA": "solder",   "patchA": "solderToSpreader",
      "regionB": "spreader", "patchB": "spreaderToSolder",
      "Rc": 2.5e-5
    },
    {
      // The same number, written the other way: a 40 um bond line of a
      // k = 1.0 W/(m K) adhesive is R = 4e-5 m^2 K/W, which SPEC-LIT (S47.11)
      // sums for you. Writing both `Rc` AND these is refused - they are two
      // spellings of one number.
      "regionA": "spreader", "patchA": "spreaderToGrease",
      "regionB": "grease",   "patchB": "greaseToSpreader",
      "thicknessLayers": [4.0e-5],
      "kappaLayers": [1.0]
    }
  ],

  "initial": { "T": 300.0 },
  "run": { "steady": true, "mode": "stress" },

  "numerics": {
    // A pure conduction matrix is symmetric INCLUDING its coupled interface
    // entries (SPEC-LIT §47.2 writes both sides from one h_G and one |Sf|),
    // and §48.3's extended check now verifies both halves - so PCG with an
    // incomplete-Cholesky preconditioner is legal here and is the right
    // choice.
    //
    // tolerance 1e-11, not the 1e-14 this file carried when it was
    // conduction-only: the per-region §8.4 residual of three of the four
    // regions floors at 9.4e-14 .. 1.6e-12 of its own initial value in f64
    // (each region's linear solve already runs its 4000-5000 iterations
    // against a Krylov space a tenth of that size), and SPEC-LIT §96.2
    // refuses to solve displacement on a region whose thermal verdict is
    // NOT met. 1e-11 is the smallest power of ten every region meets
    // (measured finals: die 9.4e-14, solder 3.7e-13, spreader 1.6e-12,
    // grease 1.7e-15). The temperature field moves by less than its last
    // printed digit; the closed-form test above is unaffected.
    "solver": "PCG",
    "preconditioner": "DIC",
    "tolerance": 1e-11,
    "maxIter": 5000
  },

  "output": { "exact": { "format": "vtu" } }
}
